#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""One cell of the cross-backend restore matrix, at the binary level.

A cell proves that a backup taken on the source backend restores, through the
store, into the target backend: the restored table has the source's items,
key schema, secondary indexes, throughput, SSE and (where the target supports
them) vector indexes, and its class is STANDARD. The target's catalog has no
`backup_index` row for the source's backup until `backup sync --adopt-store`
runs (PR 10); the cell stands in for that command by copying the prefix under
a root the target owns and seeding the row at the storage level, so it proves
the restore and not the adoption. `matrix-nightly.yml` runs the twelve plain
cells and the duplicate-key and vector cells through this committed script.

    backup-restore-matrix --source <sqlite|postgres|mongodb> --target <sqlite|postgres|mongodb|cassandra>
              [--items N] [--vector] [--duplicate-key] [--work DIR]
              [--bin-sqlite PATH] [--bin-postgres PATH] [--bin-mongodb PATH] [--bin-cassandra PATH]

Starts a source server from the backend's binary with a filesystem store at
its own root, creates a composite-key table with a GSI and an LSI (and, with
--vector, a vector index), writes --items items carrying every attribute
type, takes a backup and waits for AVAILABLE. Starts the target server with
its own empty root (it writes its own extenddb-store.json), copies the
source's backup prefix under the target root, seeds the target's
backup_index row at the storage level with the target's store id (what
`backup sync --adopt-store` will do in PR 10; the cell proves the restore,
not the adoption), restores, waits for ACTIVE, compares the item set and the
description (key schema, indexes, throughput, SSE; the table class STANDARD),
queries each GSI, and tears both servers down by PID and drops their
databases. With --duplicate-key the copied prefix is replaced by a hand-built
unordered backup whose two data files share one key, and the cell passes
when the restore fails (the target gone, no table) instead.

The two servers connect to PostgreSQL at 127.0.0.1:55432 (postgres/devpass),
MongoDB at 127.0.0.1:27117 and Cassandra at 127.0.0.1:9042, the coordinates
`matrix-nightly.yml` publishes its services on. Each server is an `init` with
its port patched, `[backup]` with short intervals and `serve --foreground`,
the test credentials provisioned through devtools/provision-test-credentials.
The MongoDB and Cassandra deployments use the backends' fixed names (the
`extenddb_catalog` and `extenddb_data` databases; the `extenddb_catalog` and
`extenddb_account_<account>` keyspaces) and drop them in `init` and
`teardown`, so two cells with a MongoDB or Cassandra role, or a cell beside
another process on those names, destroy each other's catalogs: run such cells
one at a time, as the nightly does. The MongoDB and Cassandra roles need
`pymongo` and `cassandra-driver` (requirements-matrix.txt).

Logs go under --work (default `$TMPDIR/backup-restore-matrix/<cell>`).
Exit 0 when the cell passes, 1 on an assertion failure or a setup error.
"""
